commentBody:
The wheel of time rolls on its way;

From age we learn to love;

Our hearts are warmed and stirred.

Memories fond are ever new

Whene'er we're served fondue.

@Dan,
The “for instance” you mention is the only place where you could call the path arbitrary.  The additional obstacles added by the setters makes this into a true maze. 
In my mind, each letter was a small bit of cheese that enticed the rat to continue to the maze exit. So, the H (bit) would have been visible to the rat as it “turned the corner”. (And no self-respecting lab rat would leave a bit of cheese before seeking out others.)

@Skeptical1

Agreed - the essence of crossword puzzles for me is the wordplay.  Other devices and ancillary devices are cute, but in my mind more for the constructors than for the solvers.

All that aside, this puzzle was relatively pleasant with straight forward clueing.

@Charles Peterson,
The “winding path” was the only way to solve the maze. Between the puzzle’s blocks, and the additional walls, obstacles, barriers, etc. added by the setters, a lab rat could easily have strayed off course and not found the cheese.  I think the animation immediately following the successful solve did, indeed, cheat some solvers from the added fun of the puzzle within the puzzle, but it might have depended somewhat on what came last for any given solver.  For me, 1a was my last fill, and focused as I am on getting the puzzle completed, the immediate graphics addition did prevent me from getting that part of the puzzle. Was, I disappointed?  Nah.  Instead I was amazed (as the title itself suggested) by the intricacies of this construction without reliance on a lot of nonsense fill.

Very creative!

@D 
Puzzle was great!
The book is horrible.
